Task 2: Upload Content Collection from Git Repository
Subtask 2.1: Prepare a Sample Collection from Git
Create a working directory:
mkdir -p ~/ansible-collections
cd ~/ansible-collections
Clone a sample collection repository:
git clone https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.general.git
cd community.general
Examine the collection structure:
ls -la
cat galaxy.yml
Subtask 2.2: Build the Collection
Install ansible-core if not already installed:
pip3 install ansible-core
Build the collection tarball:
ansible-galaxy collection build
Verify the built collection:
ls -la *.tar.gz
Subtask 2.3: Upload Collection via Web Interface
Return to the hub web interface in your browser.

Navigate to Collections > Upload Collection.

Upload the collection:

Click Browse and select your .tar.gz file
Add a description: "Community General Collection"
Select appropriate tags
Click Upload
Monitor the upload progress in the upload status page.

Subtask 2.4: Upload Collection via Command Line
Alternatively, you can upload using the command line:

Install the galaxy-importer tool:
pip3 install galaxy-importer
Configure authentication:
export ANSIBLE_GALAXY_SERVER_AUTOMATION_HUB_URL=http://localhost/api/automation-hub/
export ANSIBLE_GALAXY_SERVER_AUTOMATION_HUB_TOKEN=your-api-token
Upload the collection:
ansible-galaxy collection publish community-general-*.tar.gz --server automation_hub
Task 3: Upload Content Collection from Local Directory
Subtask 3.1: Create a Custom Collection
Create a new collection structure:
cd ~/ansible-collections
mkdir -p my_namespace/my_collection
cd my_namespace/my_collection
Initialize the collection:
ansible-galaxy collection init my_namespace.my_collection --init-path ~/ansible-collections/
Navigate to the collection directory:
cd ~/ansible-collections/my_namespace/my_collection
Subtask 3.2: Add Content to the Collection
Create a simple role:
mkdir -p roles/webserver/tasks
mkdir -p roles/webserver/handlers
mkdir -p roles/webserver/templates
Create the main task file:
cat > roles/webserver/tasks/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Install Apache web server
  package:
    name: httpd
    state: present
  become: yes

- name: Start and enable Apache
  service:
    name: httpd
    state: started
    enabled: yes
  become: yes
  notify: restart apache

- name: Create index.html
  template:
    src: index.html.j2
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html
  become: yes
  notify: restart apache
EOF
Create a handler:
cat > roles/webserver/handlers/main.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: restart apache
  service:
    name: httpd
    state: restarted
  become: yes
EOF
Create a template:
cat > roles/webserver/templates/index.html.j2 << 'EOF'
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>{{ ansible_hostname }}</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to {{ ansible_hostname }}</h1>
    <p>This server is managed by Ansible</p>
</body>
</html>
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Configure Collection Metadata
Edit the galaxy.yml file:
cat > galaxy.yml << 'EOF'
namespace: my_namespace
name: my_collection
version: 1.0.0
readme: README.md
authors:
  - Your Name <your.email@example.com>
description: A sample collection for web server management
license:
  - GPL-2.0-or-later
license_file: ''
tags:
  - webserver
  - apache
  - infrastructure
dependencies: {}
repository: http://example.com/repository
documentation: http://docs.example.com
homepage: http://example.com
issues: http://example.com/issues
build_ignore: []
EOF
Create a README file:
cat > README.md << 'EOF'
# My Collection

This collection provides roles and playbooks for web server management.

## Included Content

- **Roles**:
  - `webserver`: Installs and configures Apache web server

## Installation

```bash
ansible-galaxy collection install my_namespace.my_collection
Usage
- hosts: webservers
  roles:
    - my_namespace.my_collection.webserver
EOF


